  "body": "Integrates the complete SCAN production-readiness stack onto `main` and closes the staged draft-PR lineage.\n\nIncluded roadmap:\n\ncoverage/gaps \u2192 uncertainty challenger \u2192 triage ranking \u2192 layered provenance \u2192 parity scenarios/distribution \u2192 broader calibration \u2192 independent reconstruction proof \u2192 authorized runtime validation \u2192 operational hardening \u2192 v1.0 certification.\n\nThis integration also closes repository-level release loose ends:\n- root and `Anchor/` documentation now point to SCAN v1.0 instead of the stale v0.23/v0.27 checkpoint;\n- the v1.0 release index and release notes are present;\n- the final certification workflow now reruns on `main`;\n- the main release commit must be a two-parent integration merge whose tree is byte-identical to this checked source head;\n- the main release gate verifies successful broader-calibration and independent-reconstruction stage checks on that source head;\n- after all main-side certification gates pass, the workflow publishes the immutable `v1.0.0` GitHub release and refuses to move an existing tag to different bytes.\n\nThe earlier v1.0 candidate at `7b63b19932fd4c20c7599c58959202527d5dd3f0` passed 210 tests, the 9-lane Linux/macOS/Windows \u00d7 Python 3.11/3.12/3.13 matrix, and live authorized runtime validation. Subsequent repository/documentation/publication cleanup changed the source-head identity, so the current PR head is being fully recertified rather than inheriting that older certificate.\n\nAfter merge, `main` is recertified again before publication. The final GitHub release is therefore bound to the actual merge commit and sealed package bytes, not merely to the pre-merge branch head.\n\nAuthority boundaries and non-claims documented in the v1.0 certification contract remain unchanged.",
